Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.309 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  four years ago  softbank founder masayoshi sun stormed  into britain  and bought uk chip designer arm holdings  in the country's biggest ever tech deal  for 32 billion dollars coming a week  after the uk voted to leave the eu  the transaction was held by some  politicians as an example of continued  investor interest from international  companies  despite brexit mr sun declared arm to be  a crystal ball  that would guide him to make investments  in everything from artificial  intelligence to the internet of things  with arm under his control the japanese  billionaire investor  quickly convinced backers in saudi  arabia and abu dhabi to pour tens of  billions of dollars into a new  hundred billion dollar fund called the  vision fund which would focus on  startups  but the investors only joined on the  condition that a slice of arm was moved  into the fund  four years later mr sun's crystal ball  has yielded few results  under softbank's ownership arm failed to  thrive and the vision fund which became  known for a series of aggressive bets on  startups including wework  and wag hasn't turned out quite as he  would have hoped  meanwhile other chip companies did much  better than arm  one of those us-based nvidia was worth  only 30 billion dollars when softbank  acquired the uk company  but by the time nvidia decided it was  interested in buying arm in the summer  of 2020  its market value had surpassed 300  billion by september  nvidia agreed to pay up to 40 billion  dollars for arm  it's not a done deal though uk  politicians are clamoring for  commitments from nvidia to keep some of  arm's operations in britain  longer term the transaction will also  require clearance from places such as  china which may look to scupper the deal  as part of its ongoing spat with the u.s  over sensitive technology  for softbank though the crystal ball  upon which mr sun had staked the  company's future is gone  the question is what comes next for  softbank and its investors

